      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm putting together a training module for our new ED nurses here in Chicago. Can you help me draft a standard verbal de-escalation protocol for when we have patients who become highly agitated or aggressive due to distress or substance use? I need it to be clear and professional for the staff handbook.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here's a sample verbal de-escalation protocol:\n\n\"When interacting with a distressed or aggressive patient, use the following verbal de-escalation steps:\n\n1. Remain calm and composed, with a calm and gentle tone of voice.\n2. Acknowledge and validate their feelings, using phrases such as 'I can see that you're very upset' or 'I understand that you're feeling anxious'.\n3. De-escalate anxiety with empathetic statements, such as 'I'm here to support you' or 'Let's take a few deep breaths together'.\n4. Use active listening skills, focusing on the patient's words, tone, and body language.\n5. Offer choices and maintain problem-solving skills to empower the patient and reduce aggression.\n\nSpecific language examples to avoid:\n- 'Calm down'\n- 'You're going to be okay'\n- 'What's wrong with you?'\n- 'Don't be so loud'\n\nRemember, the goal of verbal de-escalation is to reduce tension and promote calm, facilitating collaborative care and safe interactions with the patient.\"\n\nThis protocol focuses on empathy, active listening, and validated statements to reduce tension and promote a sense of safety and understanding for patients.",
