    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Here's an additional section on recognizing early warning signs of escalation:\n\n**Early Warning Signs of Escalation:**\n\n\"Bother Management Begins\"\n\nRecognize that de-escalation is proactive, not reactive. By anticipating and responding to early warning signs, nurses can intervene before situations escalate:\n\n1. **Verbal Cues:**\n\t* Increased loudness or aggression\n\t* Raised tone or use of profanity\n\t* Threats or explicit expressions of anger\n\t* Abrupt or dismissive communication\n2. **Non-Verbal Cues:**\n\t* Fidgeting, pacing, or restless body language\n\t* Increased touch or invading personal space\n\t* Clenched fists, facial expressions, or visible signs of tension\n\t* Avoiding eye contact or escalating it (ejection)\n\t* Frowning, scowling, or dramatic facial expressions\n3. **Behavioral Changes:**\n\t* Becoming quieter or less verbally communicative\n\t* Suddenly becoming more agitated or seeking attention\n\t* Sweating, flushing, or other visible physiological responses\n\t* Resistance to examination, treatment, or care\n\t* Disorientation or becoming disconnected from the environment\n4. **Psychos",
